1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=-{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444365 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444365 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107272" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444365 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444365 #edb/ref 1071 "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-23 & 6 \\\\ 8 & -10\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-7.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444365 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "59c071a9-0532-5356-9a7b-f9737214176f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444365 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-7.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-23 & 6 \\\\ 8 & -10\\end{aligned}]-(-7)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 6 \\\\ 8 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-16{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 8{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 6 \\\\ 8 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-16{x}_{1}+6{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=\\frac{3}{8}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=8,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444366 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444366 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80323" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444366 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444366 #edb/ref 1071 "Given that $\\lambda =-2$ and $\\lambda =-1$ are the eigenvalues of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]$,\nfind two linearly independent eigenvectors of $A.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444366 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cc10999e-0e98-57c8-9318-5ca4b1f6c8ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444366 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the eigenvalues separately. \n\n- For $\\lambda =-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}4 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain  ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For $\\lambda =-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -12 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-12{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \nFrom the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=4{x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, two linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix $A$ are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444367 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186444360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444367 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107270" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444367 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444367 #edb/ref 1071 "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444367 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d0ad3502-048f-57c9-928b-2eb350ba84cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444367 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =4.$ So, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwith the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -9 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. \n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n3{x}_{1}-9{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444368 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186444372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444368 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "37c3a8ed-fc50-5c91-b1c0-b7f217640868" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444368 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186444365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444369 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186444368 #edb/ref 1759218604